---
/**
 * Section 02 — the freeze gate, shown as code plus the output that code
 * actually prints.
 *
 * Both blocks come from `examples/quantum_examples/qcm_freeze_check`: the call
 * shape is condensed from its `main.rs`, and the terminal block is the run's
 * own stdout. Nothing here is illustrative.
 */
import { Code } from 'astro:components';
import { rustDark, rustLight } from '../../../shiki-rust-themes.mjs';

const SHIKI = { light: rustLight, dark: rustDark };

const call = `use deep_causality_quantum::{
    CommutatorTolerance, QuantumErrorEnum, freeze_quantum,
};

// σx and σz are both declared on leg 0, so their supports intersect.
let (factors, supports) = factors_on_shared_leg(sigma_x(), sigma_z());
let tolerance = CommutatorTolerance::<FloatType>::default();

match freeze_quantum(&mut graph, &[], &factors, &supports, &tolerance, None) {
    Ok(report) => { /* report.tested_pairs(), report.worst_margin() */ }
    Err(e) => match e.0 {
        QuantumErrorEnum::CommutatorNonZero { node_j, node_k, .. } => {
            // The offending pair arrives as data, not as a string to parse.
        }
        _ => {}
    },
}`;

const output = `[1] Commuting model: σz and diag(3, -1) on leg 0
    ✓ froze cleanly; 1 commuting pair(s) checked, worst margin 0.000e0
    is_frozen() = true

[2] Non-commuting model: σx and σz on leg 0
    ✓ freeze aborted: factors at nodes 0 and 1 do not commute
    is_frozen() = false (rolled back)`;
---
<section class="qsec page" data-rise>
  <header class="sec-head">
    <p class="eyebrow eyebrow-coord">
      <span class="ix">02 / 05</span><span class="lb">Freeze</span>
    </p>
    <h2>The Markov condition as a gate</h2>
    <p class="lede">
      <code>freeze_quantum</code> runs the check at the boundary where a graph
      stops being editable. A model that fails never becomes frozen.
    </p>
  </header>

  <div class="split">
    <div>
      <Code code={call} lang="rust" themes={SHIKI} />
    </div>
    <div>
      <Code code={output} lang="text" themes={SHIKI} />
      <p class="cap">
        Output of <code>cargo run --release -p quantum_examples --example
        qcm_freeze_check</code>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="panel corner-brackets body-panel rise">
    <p class="body">
      The check walks factor pairs and skips any whose supports are disjoint,
      because those carry no obligation. For a pair that does intersect, both
      factors are embedded onto the union of their legs by
      <code>embed_on_legs</code>, the commutator is formed there, and its
      Frobenius norm is compared against a forward-error threshold.
    </p>

    <ul class="nums">
      <li>‖[ρⱼ, ρₖ]‖_F ≤ C · (‖ρⱼ‖·budgetₖ + ‖ρₖ‖·budgetⱼ + 2·γₙ·‖ρⱼ‖·‖ρₖ‖)</li>
      <li>γₙ = n·u / (1 − n·u), with n the embedded dimension</li>
      <li>Defaults: safety factor C = 8, unit roundoff u = ε, per-node budget u·‖ρ‖_F</li>
    </ul>

    <p class="body">
      The threshold is condition-driven rather than linear in graph depth. A
      model constructed through many operations can hand a larger per-node
      budget to <code>with_budget</code> instead of loosening the whole check.
    </p>

    <p class="body">
      The check is sound and may be incomplete: it never accepts a pair whose
      commutator exceeds the threshold, and it does not claim to catch every
      pair that a tighter analysis would reject. On failure the graph rolls back
      to its dynamic state and the structured error survives the trip through
      the engine's own error channel, so the caller reads node indices rather
      than a formatted message.
    </p>

    <p class="body">
      Passing input and output systems as the last argument adds a second gate:
      C₃-exclusion faithfulness, derived from the frozen graph's own
      reachability.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p class="more">
    <a href="/qcm/">How the tolerance and the faithfulness check work</a>
    <a href="/examples/qcm-freeze-check/">Run it</a>
  </p>
</section>

<style>
  .qsec { max-width: var(--w-panel); }
  .sec-head { margin-bottom: var(--space-6); }
  .sec-head h2 { margin: var(--space-3) 0 var(--space-3); }
  .lede { color: var(--fg-1); max-width: var(--measure-lede); margin: 0; }

  /* minmax(0, 1fr) guards the code columns: a plain 1fr cannot shrink below
     its content's min-content width, and an unbroken code line blows out the
     grid. */
  .split {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: var(--space-4);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-6);
  }
  @media (min-width: 900px) {
    .split { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(0, 1fr); gap: var(--space-5); }
  }
  .cap { color: var(--fg-1); font-size: var(--t-body-sm); margin: var(--space-3) 0 0; }

  .body-panel { padding: var(--space-5); }
  .body { color: var(--fg-1); font-size: var(--t-body-sm); max-width: var(--measure); margin: 0 0 var(--space-4); }
  .body:last-child { margin-bottom: 0; }

  .nums { list-style: none; margin: 0 0 var(--space-4); padding: 0; display: grid; gap: var(--space-2); }
  .nums li {
    color: var(--fg-1);
    padding-left: var(--space-3);
    border-left: 1px solid var(--line-2);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--t-mono-sm);
    max-width: var(--measure);
  }

  .more {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: var(--space-4);
    margin: var(--space-6) 0 0;
    padding-top: var(--space-3);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--line-1);
    font-size: var(--t-body-sm);
  }
</style>
